WEBVTT

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:04.122
Welcome to Module 13, Introduction to Spark SQL.

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:07.032
In earlier modules, we used PostgreSQL to learn relational thinking.

00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:11.607
In this module, we keep that same thinking, but we move the execution environment to Spark.

00:00:11.607 --> 00:00:18.872
Spark SQL lets us write familiar SQL while Spark plans and executes the work across distributed data.

00:00:18.872 --> 00:00:21.332
The key question is not only whether the query is correct.

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:28.467
We also ask what data is scanned, what has to move across workers, and what one output row represents.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:33.012
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:33.012 --> 00:00:35.862
a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files.

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:43.517
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:46.617
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:52.467
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:55.437
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:55.437 --> 00:00:58.240
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
